## Assumptions:

[C0] : O is the circumcenter of the circle ●ABC
[C1] : AO ⟂ AT

## Numerical checks

[N0] : ▲BCO has the same orientation as ▲BCO


# Goals

∠(AB,AT) = ∠(BC,AC) : Proved [8]


# Proof:

000. | O is the circumcenter of the circle ●ABC [C0] =(r72 Disassembling a circle)> BO = AO [0]
001. | BO = AO [0] =(r13 Isosceles triangle equal angles)> ∠(AB,AO) = ∠(BO,AB) [1]
002. | O is the circumcenter of the circle ●ABC [C0] =(r72 Disassembling a circle)> BO = CO [2]
003. | BO = AO [0], BO = CO [2] =(AR Deduction)> AO = CO [3]
004. | AO = CO [3] =(r13 Isosceles triangle equal angles)> ∠(AC,AO) = ∠(CO,AC) [4]
005. | BO = CO [2] =(AR Deduction)> BO:CO = CO:BO [5]
006. | ∠(BO,CO) = ∠(BO,CO) [T0], BO:CO = CO:BO [5], ▲BCO has the same orientation as ▲BCO [N0] =(r63 SAS Similarity of triangles (Reverse))> ▲BCO ≅ ▲CBO [6]
007. | ▲BCO has the same orientation as ▲BCO [N0], ▲BCO ≅ ▲CBO [6] =(r53 Properties of similar triangles (Reverse))> ∠(BC,BO) = ∠(CO,BC) [7]
008. | ∠(AB,AO) = ∠(BO,AB) [1], ∠(AC,AO) = ∠(CO,AC) [4], ∠(BC,BO) = ∠(CO,BC) [7], AO ⟂ AT [C1] =(AR Deduction)> ∠(AB,AT) = ∠(BC,AC) [8]


# Appendix: AR deductions:



003. | Ratio Chasing to prove AO = CO:
| Premise BO = AO gives a linear equation with coefficients {AO²: 1, BO²: -1}
| Premise BO = CO gives a linear equation with coefficients {BO²: 1, CO²: -1}
| Then {AO²: 1, BO²: -1} + 1 x {BO²: 1, CO²: -1}
|    = {AO²: 1, CO²: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AO²: 1, CO²: -1} that gives AO = CO



005. | Ratio Chasing to prove BO:CO = CO:BO:
| Premise BO = CO gives a linear equation with coefficients {BO²: 1, CO²: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {BO²: 2, CO²: -2} that gives BO:CO = CO:BO



008. | Angle Chasing to prove ∠(AB,AT) = ∠(BC,AC):
| Premise ∠(AB,AO) = ∠(BO,AB) gives a linear equation with coefficients {AB: -2, AO: 1, BO: 1}
| Premise ∠(AC,AO) = ∠(CO,AC) gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: -2, AO: 1, CO: 1}
| Then {AB: -1, AO: 1/2, BO: 1/2} + 1/2 x {AC: -2, AO: 1, CO: 1}
|    = {AB: -1, AO: 1, BO: 1/2, AC: -1, CO: 1/2}
| Premise ∠(BC,BO) = ∠(CO,BC) gives a linear equation with coefficients {BC: -2, BO: 1, CO: 1}
| Then {AB: -1, AO: 1, BO: 1/2, AC: -1, CO: 1/2} + -1/2 x {BC: -2, BO: 1, CO: 1}
|    = {AB: -1, AO: 1, AC: -1, BC: 1}
| Premise AO ⟂ AT gives a linear equation with coefficients {AO: 1, AT: -1}
| Then {AB: -1, AO: 1, AC: -1, BC: 1} + -1 x {AO: 1, AT: -1}
|    = {AB: -1, AC: -1, BC: 1, AT: 1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AB: -1, AC: -1, BC: 1, AT: 1} that gives ∠(AB,AT) = ∠(BC,AC)

End of proof.

